{
 "_comment": "The Coronado Islands as land, for the grids GMRT left them out of (BRIEFING section 55; docs/design/geometry.md, 'The Coronado Islands'). GMRT's 90 m grid has only South Coronado as land (Middle Coronado a shoal whose shallowest stored cell is 5.2 m deep, North Coronado 22 m and more) and its 400 m grid none, so rays refracted over shoals where islands stand and the map drew open water there. forecast.raytrace.burn_islands raises a grid's cells to land where at least a quarter of the cell is island, at the island's height or the grid's own elevation, whichever is higher; the grids as fetched are not edited, so fetching them again keeps the islands.",
 "source": "Esri World Imagery (Vivid: WorldView-2 2023-01-31 and 2022-01-19, 0.5 m, stated accuracy 5 m, at North Coronado's south-east end; the captures in force at zoom 17 elsewhere)",
 "measured": "2026-11-15",
 "method": "Land by colour at zoom 17 (about 1 m a pixel): red over blue by more than 8 (sand, rock, scrub), every water pixel not joined to the open sea counted as land, a 12 m opening to drop rocks awash, components of 200 m2 and more; outlines simplified to 2 m. The method of BRIEFING sections 52-54. South Coronado's imaged south-east tip (spots.json) lies 3 m inside this outline, North Coronado's imaged ends 0.2 and 1.5 m outside theirs.",
 "registration": "Copernicus GLO-30 (TanDEM-X radar topography, land over 1 m) agrees with the imagery on the main islands within 0.02 degrees of edge from every Coronado break; it does not carry Middle Coronado (6,000 m2 over 1 m, against 13,000 imaged) or North Coronado's south-east islet (2,565 m2), both under its 30 m posting.",
 "heights": "height_m: Copernicus GLO-30's median over the island's outline, never under 5 m (Middle Coronado, which GLO-30 does not resolve).",
